The {{nihongo|'''Okaiken Railways'''|岡井県鉄道|Okaiken Tetsudō}}, commonly referred to as '''OR''' or the '''OR Group''', is the state-owned national railway system of [[Okaiken]]. Unlike the fully privatized railway models seen in other nations, OR is structured as an Imperial Crown Corporation, jointly owned by the Okaiken Government and the National Pension Fund.

The network is divided geographically among three major operating subsidiaries corresponding to the nation's three main landmasses: '''Harujima''' (Northwest), '''Ishijima''' (Northeast), and '''Yagikoshu''' (South). While these entities have operational autonomy, they are centrally managed by the OR Group Directorate to ensure national cohesion, fare stabilization, and strategic infrastructure maintenance.

The OR Group operates the high-speed [[Shinkansen]] network, intercity limited expresses, and the urban commuter networks in major metropolises including the capital, [[Okami]].

==History==
===Okaiken National Railways (1949–1987)===
{{main|Okaiken National Railways}}
The predecessor to OR was the {{nihongo|'''Okaiken National Railways'''|岡井県国有鉄道|Okaiken Kokuyū Tetsudō}}, commonly abbreviated as '''ONR''', which operated from 1949 to 1987. ONR was established as a public corporation on June 1, 1949, during the post-war occupation period, consolidating the previously government-owned railway assets into a single quasi-independent entity.

During the 1950s and 1960s, ONR was instrumental in Okaiken's rapid economic growth. The agency oversaw the electrification of major trunk lines, the modernization of rolling stock, and the planning of the revolutionary [[Shinkansen]] network. The first Shinkansen line—the [[Harujima Shinkansen]]—opened in 1966 under ONR's direction, marking Okaiken as the first nation in Adonia to operate dedicated high-speed rail.

However, by the 1970s, ONR faced mounting financial difficulties. The agency had accumulated significant debt through aggressive network expansion, including the politically motivated sea routing of the Harujima Shinkansen (known as the "Great LDP Snub") and the construction of unprofitable rural lines. Labor relations also deteriorated; the ONR workers' union, the [[National Railway Workers' Union (Okaiken)|Kokutetsu Rōsō]], engaged in numerous strikes and work-to-rule campaigns that disrupted service and damaged public perception.

By 1980, ONR's long-term debt had reached ¥28 trillion, threatening the nation's fiscal stability. The government explored various reform options, ultimately deciding against full privatization—which would have left rural communities without service—in favor of a hybrid restructuring model.

===The Reform of 1987===
On April 1, 1987, the government reorganized ONR into the Okaiken Railways Group. Unlike the privatization models implemented in other nations, OR was structured as an Imperial Crown Corporation. Ownership was transferred to a holding company held 51% by the Imperial Treasury and 49% by the National Pension Fund, ensuring that profits are reinvested into the network or used to bolster the nation's social security rather than distributed to private shareholders.

The reform also addressed the labor situation. While workforce reduction was necessary—employment dropped from 400,000 at ONR's peak to approximately 180,000—the restructuring avoided the mass dismissals seen elsewhere through voluntary retirement programs and reassignment to affiliated companies. The remaining ONR debt was assumed by the national government rather than transferred to the new OR Group, allowing the railway to begin operations on sound financial footing.

===Modern developments===
Since privatization, OR has focused on modernization and efficiency improvements. The 1990s saw the extension of the [[Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen]] from Toyozawa to Kagami, opening in 1993. The network has embraced digital technology, implementing the [[Oka-Pass]] electronic ticketing system in 2001 and automated train operations on select lines.

The 2011 [[Jutsu Restoration]] brought renewed government attention to infrastructure, with the OR Group receiving additional capital for the ongoing Kii-to-Aomori extension of the Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen, projected for completion in 2035. In 2016, OR introduced the H200 Series Shinkansen trains on the Harujima line, capable of operating at {{convert|320|km/h|mph|abbr=on}}.

