===Harujima Shinkansen (OR Harujima)===
The {{nihongo|'''Harujima Shinkansen'''|春島新幹線}} is the oldest and busiest line in the system, opened on May 28, 1966. It connects the northern port of [[Shinagawa]] (population 2.3 million) with the capital city of [[Okami]] (population 8.3 million) and continues south to the central junction of [[Toyozawa]] (population 6.1 million). It is the primary artery for business travel and generates approximately 65% of OR's Shinkansen revenue.

Three service patterns operate on the line:
* '''Nozomi:''' Express service stopping only at major cities; 2 hours 31 minutes end-to-end
* '''Nanpu:''' Semi-express service with 18 intermediate stops; 3 hours 18 minutes
* '''Hikari:''' All-stations service; 4 hours 35 minutes

The line is notable for its controversial inland routing through [[Mie Prefecture]] during the 1960s—a political decision known as the {{nihongo|"Great LDP Snub"|大自民党冷遇|Dai Jiyūmin Tō Reigyō}}—which bypassed the more populous coastal cities in favor of politically loyal rural districts. This decision continues to impact regional development patterns today.

===Ishijima Shinkansen (OR Ishijima)===
The {{nihongo|'''Ishijima Shinkansen'''|石島新幹線}} opened in 1982, running along the western spine of [[Ishijima]] island. It connects the northwestern metropolis of [[Aizawa]] (population 2 million) with [[Toyozawa]] via the industrial hub of [[Minatojima]] (population 1.25 million). The line is famous for its scenic coastal sections, particularly the {{nihongo|Seto Coast Corridor|瀬戸海岸回廊}} where trains run within 50 meters of the coastline for 87 kilometers.

Two service patterns operate:
* '''Shiokaze:''' Express service; 3 hours 15 minutes with 4 stops
* '''Yamabiko:''' All-stations service; 4 hours 30 minutes

===Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen (OR Yagikoshu)===
The {{nihongo|'''Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen'''|南八木甲州新幹線}} extends southward from [[Toyozawa]], penetrating the mountainous terrain of [[Yagikoshu]] island. The operational section connects Toyozawa to [[Kii]] (population 195,000) via [[Mito]] (population 1.9 million). An extension to [[Aomori]] (population 215,000) is currently under construction, with completion projected for 2035.

The line presented significant engineering challenges, including the {{nihongo|Tanzawa Tunnel|丹沢トンネル}}—at 26.5 kilometers, the longest railway tunnel in Okaiken—and the {{nihongo|Great Seto Bridge|大瀬戸大橋}} crossing into Yagikoshu.

Two service patterns operate:
* '''Uzushio:''' Express service; 3 hours 45 minutes from Toyozawa to Kii
* '''Tsunagi:''' All-stations service; 5 hours 00 minutes

===Toyozawa Junction Configuration===
[[File:Toyozawa Station Y-Junction.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Y-junction configuration at Toyozawa Station, where all three Shinkansen lines converge.]]
[[Toyozawa Station]] features a unique 10-track, 5-island platform configuration with a Y-junction beyond the station throat. This design allows for platform splitting and combining operations:

* '''Split Operations:''' 16-car Nozomi trains arriving from Shinagawa on a single track split on the platform, with the front 8 cars proceeding to Aizawa and the rear 8 cars to Kii via separate junctions
* '''Combine Operations:''' 8-car trains from Aizawa and Kii arrive on the same track, couple on the platform, and depart as a 16-car train to Shinagawa

This configuration maximizes platform efficiency and allows direct through-services between all three lines without requiring passengers to change trains at Toyozawa.

==Conventional Rail Network==
Beyond the Shinkansen, OR operates an extensive network of conventional railway lines totaling approximately {{Convert|17,392|km|abbr=on}}. These lines utilize the narrow {{RailGauge|1067mm}} gauge, a legacy of early railway development influenced by continental engineers from the [[Kingdom of Zong]].

===Intercity and Limited Express Services===
OR operates numerous limited express services (''tokkyū'') connecting major cities not served by the Shinkansen network. These trains, often named after natural features or historical references, provide comfortable long-distance travel at speeds up to {{Convert|130|km/h|mph|abbr=on}}. Notable limited express routes include:

* '''Katsura:''' Connects Okami with coastal cities along the western Harujima seaboard
* '''Shirakawa:''' Links Toyozawa with the southern Yagikoshu prefectures
* '''Asuka:''' Premium commuter service between Aizawa and Minatojima

===Commuter Rail===
In major metropolitan areas, OR operates dense commuter networks that form the backbone of urban transportation:

* '''Okami Commuter Network:''' 12 lines radiating from [[Okami Central Station]], serving over 8 million daily passengers
* '''Toyozawa Urban Network:''' Integrates with the [[Toyozawa Metro]] at major interchange stations
* '''Aizawa Metropolitan Network:''' Connects the Ishijima financial district with suburban residential areas

These commuter networks are characterized by high-frequency service during peak hours, with trains operating at intervals as short as two minutes on the busiest corridors.

===Freight Operations===
[[OR Freight]] operates the nation's rail freight network, transporting approximately 40 million tonnes of cargo annually. Major commodities include automotive parts, industrial chemicals, and agricultural products. The freight division utilizes dedicated freight lines in urban areas and shares tracks with passenger services on rural routes during off-peak hours.

The primary freight hub is the [[Okami Freight Terminal]], which handles 12 million tonnes annually. Secondary hubs include [[Aizawa Inland Port]] and [[Toyozawa Distribution Center]], both connected to container port facilities.

==Major Stations and Terminals==
OR operates 2,847 stations nationwide, ranging from unmanned rural halts to massive metropolitan terminals handling millions of passengers daily. The network's design emphasizes seamless transfers between Shinkansen, limited express, and commuter services.

