===Conventional Fleet===
OR's conventional fleet comprises approximately 8,500 vehicles operating on narrow-gauge lines. Key series include:

* '''[[2000 Series]]''' (1995–present): Workhorse commuter trains on urban networks; 2,400 cars in service
* '''[[2500 Series]]''' (2008–present): Modern commuter trains with enhanced accessibility features; 1,200 cars in service
* '''[[KiHa 100 Series]]''' (1990–present): Diesel multiple units for non-electrified rural lines; 450 cars in service
* '''[[3500 Series]]''' (2018–present): Latest generation limited express trains; 180 cars in service
* '''[[KiHa 200 Series]]''' (2020–present): Replacement diesel fleet for rural lines; under procurement

Following the Nakayama incident, all conventional rolling stock manufactured before 2010 is being retrofitted with:
* Enhanced door sensors capable of detecting 3mm obstructions (previously 10mm)
* Emergency intercoms accessible from platform level
* Cabin cameras for driver monitoring

===Freight Fleet===
[[OR Freight]] operates approximately 500 locomotives and 8,000 freight cars:

* '''[[EH500 Class]]''' (2000–present): Dual-voltage electric locomotives for mainline freight; 180 units
* '''[[DF200 Class]]''' (1994–present): Diesel locomotives for non-electrified routes; 120 units
* '''[[M250 Series]]''' (2002–present): Electric freight multiple units for high-speed parcel services; 40 sets

===Depot Facilities===
Major maintenance depots are located at:
* '''Okami Works:''' Primary Shinkansen heavy maintenance facility
* '''Aizawa Works:''' Ishijima regional maintenance center
* '''Toyozawa Works:''' Central inspection facility for all Shinkansen lines
* '''Akita Works:''' Conventional fleet maintenance for northern Harujima

==International Connections==
OR maintains operational links with neighboring railway systems:

===Yanbaru Railways===
Through the [[Yomitan Treaty]] framework, OR coordinates with [[Yanbaru Railways]] on cross-border services. The primary connection point is the [[Shinagawa–Yomitan Ferry Terminal]], where passengers can transfer to Yanbarun trains serving the northern archipelago. Joint ticketing arrangements allow seamless travel between Okami and Yomitan.

===Zong State Railways===
Historical ties with [[Zong]] continue through technical cooperation agreements. OR provides consulting services for Zong's high-speed rail projects, while Zong exports rolling stock components to Okaiken. 

==Technology and Innovation==
OR has been at the forefront of railway technology since the Shinkansen's introduction in 1966.

===Signaling and Control===
All OR lines utilize the [[Automatic Train Control]] (ATC) system, which automatically applies brakes if a train exceeds permitted speed. The Shinkansen network employs an advanced digital ATC system capable of controlling train spacing to within 90 meters at maximum speed.

Since 2015, OR has been implementing [[Communications-Based Train Control]] (CBTC) on urban commuter lines, allowing for closer train spacing and increased capacity. The Okami Commuter Network was fully converted to CBTC in 2022, increasing peak-hour capacity by 15%.

===Ticketing Systems===
The [[Oka-Pass]] smart card system, introduced in 2001, is accepted on all OR trains and integrates with municipal transit systems in Okami, Toyozawa, Aizawa, and 47 other cities. Over 85 million cards have been issued, making it one of the most widely used transit cards in Adonia.

In 2019, OR introduced mobile Oka-Pass functionality, allowing passengers to use smartphones for fare payment. As of 2025, 62% of fare transactions are conducted via mobile devices.

===Environmental Initiatives===
OR has committed to carbon neutrality by 2040. Key initiatives include:
* '''Electrification expansion:''' 94% of track kilometers are electrified, with the remaining 6% served by hybrid diesel-electric units
* '''Solar power generation:''' Solar panels installed on station rooftops and depot facilities generate 180 MW, approximately 15% of OR's electricity consumption
* '''Regenerative braking:''' All Shinkansen and modern conventional trains recover braking energy, feeding it back into the power grid

